GitHub Pages (What & Why)

GitHub Pages is a static website hosting service provided by GitHub.

👉 What it does

It hosts:

HTML, CSS, JS
Static assets (images, fonts)
👉 What it does NOT do
No backend (Node.js, Java, DB, etc.)
No server-side rendering unless pre-built (like Next.js static export)
🔧 How it works internally
You push code to a GitHub repo
GitHub picks a branch/folder:
main / gh-pages / /docs

It serves files via CDN at:

https://username.github.io/repo-name/
🧠 Types of GitHub Pages
1. User/Org site
username.github.io
2. Project site
username.github.io/project-name
🔥 Real Use Cases
Portfolio (perfect for you)
Documentation sites
Static blogs (Jekyll, Hugo)
Frontend apps (React, Next static export)
⚠️ Limitation
No API execution → must use external backend (Node, Firebase, Supabase)